  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways an enzyme-like particle can show enhanced motion in a solution filled with many small molecules. In both cases, the blue shape represents the active particle and the red dots are surrounding solute molecules.\n\nOn the left, “catalysis-induced enhanced diffusion” is illustrated as a local, activity-driven effect. The particle appears to bind or interact with nearby yellow molecules, and its path is shown as a jagged random walk before and after catalysis. Curved arrows near the particle suggest conformational changes or reaction steps, and the velocity arrow indicates that these events increase its effective motion without a preferred external direction.\n\nOn the right, “gradient-induced enhanced diffusion” shows a different mechanism: the particle moves preferentially along a concentration gradient of surrounding molecules. The red dots become denser toward the right, matching the large colored arrow labeled concentration gradient. Here the velocity arrow points in the same direction, implying directed drift rather than only stronger random motion. Smaller curved arrows again suggest local interactions or shape changes, but the main visible message is that a spatial gradient biases movement.\n\nOverall, the figure contrasts isotropic activity-enhanced diffusion with gradient-driven, directional transport.",
